"The same node types in a graph that declares no federation link. Federation is opt-in and absence is the default, so nothing is synthesized however many nodes the graph declares; what an author wrote still composes, an authored key being a fact about the type rather than a consequence of the opt-in. Stated twice: the synthesis holds no row, and the directive anchor holds the author's key and nothing minted beside it. Resolvable is left out of the claim because an authored key that does not spell it records absence rather than the default, which is a separate fact from the one this document is about."
type Film @table(name: "film") @node @key(fields: "title") {
  id: ID
  title: String
}

# A node with no key of its own, which in a linked graph would be given one.
type Language @table(name: "language") @node {
  id: ID
  name: String
}

extend type Query {
  films: [Film!]!
  languages: [Language!]!
}

extend schema @assertAbsent(relation: "graphitron_synthesized_federation_key", rows: """
  type_name
  Film
  Language
  """)

extend schema @assertContains(relation: "graphitron_directive_application", rows: """
  coordinate, directive_name, ordinal, origin
  Film,       key,            0,       AUTHORED
  """)

extend schema @assertAbsent(relation: "graphitron_directive_application", rows: """
  coordinate, directive_name, origin
  Language,   key,            MINTED
  """)
